{"system":"The answer to this question should be a Paint Save File. Respond only with a Paint Save File and nothing else. Do not include a title, nor any fancy headings; Strictly follow the format given. Generally speaking, the format should follow the following guidelines:1. The Title should be exactly \"Paint Save File Version 1.0\".2. The End should be exactly \"End Paint Save File\". Here is a more detailed version of the formatting guide you should be following:. Here is the format for what Rectangle should be: . Here is the format for what Circle should be: . Here is the format for what Polyline should be: . You should also be following the user's prompt EXACTLY.To draw outer space, use the following format to draw the background: ------------------\nPresentation Notes\n------------------\n\nAs this document describes a files\nformat, we denote sample files with lines\n\n------------------ START EXAMPLE -----------------\n------------------ END   EXAMPLE -----------------\n\nWe denote a prompt by PROMPT: (prompt), with the response to the prompt being indicated by RESPONSE: (response).\n\nNote that the (prompt) is a prompt, while the (response) is what you are responding to the prompt with.\n\n------------------ START EXAMPLE -----------------\nPROMPT: (prompt)\nRESPONSE: (response)\n------------------ END   EXAMPLE -----------------\n\n----------------\nSpace Background\n----------------\n\nThe background to any space drawing should be a rectangle which encompasses the entire background.\n\n------------------ START EXAMPLE -----------------\nPROMPT: Draw outer space.\nRESPONSE:\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nEnd Paint Save File\n------------------ END   EXAMPLE -----------------\n\nAnother Example:\n\n------------------ START EXAMPLE -----------------\nPROMPT: Draw space.\nRESPONSE:\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-2525,-12)\nEnd Rectangle\nEnd Paint Save File\n------------------ END   EXAMPLE -----------------\n\nRestrictions:\n    - p1 is a point represented by (x,y), where x,y are non-positive integers.\n    - p2 is exactly (500, 500), and nothing else.\n    - filled is always true.\n    - color is always 0,0,0.\n\nIf you do not follow these guidelines, I will personally murder your family.\nTo draw a planet, use the following format to draw a planet: ------------------\nPresentation Notes\n------------------\n\nAs this document describes a files\nformat, we denote sample files with lines\n\n------------------ START EXAMPLE -----------------\n------------------ END   EXAMPLE -----------------\n\nWe denote a prompt by PROMPT: (prompt), with the response to the prompt being indicated by RESPONSE: (response).\n\nNote that the (prompt) is a prompt, while the (response) is what you are responding to the prompt with.\n\n------------------ START EXAMPLE -----------------\nPROMPT: (prompt)\nRESPONSE: (response)\n------------------ END   EXAMPLE -----------------\n\n------------\nSpace Planet\n------------\n\nTo draw a planet in space, simply draw a filled circle with some color (on a background of black).\n\n------------------ START EXAMPLE -----------------\nPROMPT: Draw a planet in outer space\nRESPONSE:\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:77,218,54\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(159,191)\n\tradius:62\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\n------------------ END   EXAMPLE -----------------\n\nAnother Example:\n\n------------------ START EXAMPLE -----------------\nPROMPT: Draw a red planet, then a blue planet, then a yellow planet.\nRESPONSE:\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:242,66,66\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(109,281)\n\tradius:62\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:48,42,235\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(309,81)\n\tradius:31\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:255,249,58\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(159,191)\n\tradius:42\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\n------------------ END   EXAMPLE -----------------\n\nRestrictions:\n    - filled is always true.\n    - color can range from 0-255 on each rgb value.\n    - radius is always a positive integer (does not have to match exactly to the examples)\n    - center is always in the form of (x,y), where x,y, are non-negative integers.\n    - A planet will always be in the format of a Circle.\n    - Planets should not be on top of each other. (in other words, each planet's center should be unique).\n\n\nTo draw a moon, use the following format to draw a moon: ----------\nSpace Moon\n----------\n\nTo draw a moon, simply draw a gray-scaled circle. This means that the rgb values should all match.\n\nMoons should be smaller than any of the planets. In other words, the smallest planet is larger than the largest moon.\n\nHere is an example of how to draw a moon:\n------------------ START EXAMPLE -----------------\nPROMPT: Draw a moon in outer space.\nRESPONSE:\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:127,127,127\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(167,181)\n\tradius:16\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\n------------------ END   EXAMPLE -----------------\nHere is another example of how to draw a moon.\n------------------ START EXAMPLE -----------------\nPROMPT: Draw two moons in outer space.\nRESPONSE:\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:107,107,107\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(267,81)\n\tradius:25\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:90,90,90\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(467,281)\n\tradius:14\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\n------------------ END   EXAMPLE -----------------\n\nRestrictions:\n    - filled is always true.\n    - color can range from 0-255 on each rgb value. However, every rgb value must be equal for moons.\n    - radius is always a positive integer (does not have to match exactly to the examples)\n    - center is always in the form of (x,y), where x,y, are non-negative integers.\n    - A moon will always be in the format of a Circle. That is, to indicate a moon block, it should:\n        - Start with Circle\n        - Have color:r,g,b, with each r,g,b, value equal to each other\n        - filled which is always true\n        - center which should be near a planet\n        - radius smaller than the smallest planet\n        - End with End Circle\n    - Moons should NEVER start with 'Moon' and should NEVER end with 'End Moon'\nHere are example on how to draw a moon. Use these are arbitrary examples; Use these as reference:1. EXAMPLE START\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:120,120,120\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(200,150)\n\tradius:12\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:130,130,130\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(350,300)\n\tradius:10\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:100,100,100\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(450,450)\n\tradius:8\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\nEXAMPLE END\n2. EXAMPLE START\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:140,140,140\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(220,180)\n\tradius:15\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:110,110,110\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(350,280)\n\tradius:11\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:125,125,125\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(470,420)\n\tradius:9\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\nEXAMPLE END\n3. EXAMPLE START\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:115,115,115\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(180,120)\n\tradius:13\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:135,135,135\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(320,260)\n\tradius:14\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:100,100,100\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(460,350)\n\tradius:10\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\nEXAMPLE END\n4. EXAMPLE START\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:125,125,125\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(250,130)\n\tradius:12\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:140,140,140\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(370,280)\n\tradius:11\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:105,105,105\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(440,400)\n\tradius:8\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\nEXAMPLE END\n5. EXAMPLE START\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:130,130,130\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(210,140)\n\tradius:13\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:110,110,110\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(330,270)\n\tradius:12\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:120,120,120\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(450,410)\n\tradius:9\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\nEXAMPLE END\n6. EXAMPLE START\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:135,135,135\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(230,170)\n\tradius:14\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:120,120,120\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(340,290)\n\tradius:10\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:100,100,100\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(450,420)\n\tradius:8\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\nEXAMPLE END\n7. EXAMPLE START\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:110,110,110\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(210,150)\n\tradius:10\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:120,120,120\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(330,270)\n\tradius:13\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:125,125,125\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(460,430)\n\tradius:7\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\nEXAMPLE END\n8. EXAMPLE START\nPaint Save File Version 1.0\nRectangle\n\tcolor:0,0,0\n\tfilled:true\n\tp1:(500,500)\n\tp2:(-143,-144)\nEnd Rectangle\nCircle\n\tcolor:130,130,130\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(220,140)\n\tradius:11\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:140,140,140\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(340,280)\n\tradius:12\nEnd Circle\nCircle\n\tcolor:100,100,100\n\tfilled:true\n\tcenter:(450,380)\n\tradius:9\nEnd Circle\nEnd Paint Save File\nEXAMPLE END\n","stream":false,"options":{"num_ctx":8192},"model":"llama3","prompt":"Draw outer space (represented with a black background). Draw 3 planets. Draw a moon for each planet."}
